 1 big thing: The AI agent race is here 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The long-promised personal AI assistant is finally arriving, with a suddenly crowded field of agents offering to run pieces of your everyday life.

 Why it matters: For years, tech's "personal assistants" were little more than voice-controlled search boxes. Rapid advances in AI are finally giving them the memory, judgment and autonomy to act on our behalf.

 Catch up quick: Chatbots answer questions. AI agents can actually do things — shopping, making calls, booking tickets, managing calendars and navigating the web.

 The agentic model first took off in coding and other professional work. Now it's spilling into the far more intimate terrain of everyday life. The intrigue: OpenAI is expected to enter the market soon after hiring the creator of OpenClaw — a breakout hit among AI power users earlier this year — to "drive the next generation of personal agents."

 Driving the news: A consumer-agent race has materialized almost overnight, with startups and tech giants converging on the same basic idea: persistent digital proxies that know your life.

 Instinct became an early sensation by connecting to users' email, messages, screen, audio and location — then proactively texting or calling to help run their lives. It recently added its own email addresses and the ability to make phone calls, and is now in talks for a $10 billion valuation . Grok Bot, launched by Elon Musk's SpaceXAI in August, gives persistent agents their own cloud computers, lets them sign into users' apps and keeps them working around the clock. Meta's Muse brought the concept to a broader consumer audience this month, wrapping similar autonomy in a polished app, a customizable avatar and deep integration with Meta's ecosystem. Apple's Siri — the grandmother of personal assistants — is finally getting its AI overhaul , bringing far more context and capability to an assistant already built into hundreds of millions of devices. The big picture: The battle to become your personal agent is also a battle to become the keeper of your personal information and data.

 The more an agent knows about your preferences, relationships, schedule, spending and routines, the more useful it becomes. Between the lines: Becoming someone's default agent could be one of the most valuable relationships in consumer technology.

 People are likely to share far more with an AI that manages their lives than they ever did with a search engine. That gives the company behind it extraordinary insight — and potentially influence — over what users see, buy, book and do. Yes, but: Nobody yet knows what price consumers will pay for that convenience, both in terms of cost and privacy.

 Apple says some server-based Apple Intelligence features, including the upgraded Siri, will have usage limits , with expanded access potentially tied to paid iCloud plans. Meta is offering free and paid Muse tiers while pitching the product as more privacy-conscious than its earlier AI offerings. 
 
