 2. Trump&#x27;s call for &quot;AI Force&quot; prompts scramble 
 
 By Maria Curi 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photo: Alex Grimm/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President Trump's announcement over the weekend that he's forming an "AI Force," modeled on the Space Force he launched in his first term, caught advisers by surprise, sources said.

 Officials and executives inside and outside the administration are now jockeying to be Trump's AI czar, which he said he'll name soon. The czar will likely be a seasoned surrogate with Silicon Valley ties who can spearhead an AI rebranding effort, the sources said. Why it matters: The president is seeking to shift the debate over AI safety to economic grounds, emphasizing the benefits of a multitrillion-dollar infrastructure buildout. He's trying to recast advocates of a slowdown as "doomers." 

 Zoom in: Potential investing conflicts may lead Trump to turn to insiders, perhaps with other roles in the administration. Among the names to watch:

 Scott Kupor, director of the Office of Personnel Management. Kupor has a degree in public policy and was Andreessen Horowitz's first employee. Sriram Krishnan, former senior White House AI policy adviser. Krishnan also worked at a16z and carried the torch for former AI czar David Sacks inside the White House after Sacks left earlier this year. Chamath Palihapitiya, Social Capital founder, CEO of 8090 and co-host of the " All-In Podcast " with Sacks and others. Sean Cairncross, White House national cyber director. Cairncross played a role in the administration's handling of the release of Anthropic's Mythos. Emil Michael, Pentagon CTO and under secretary for research and engineering. Michael was the face of a high-profile fight earlier this year with Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei. Between the lines: An issue for many candidates, especially those with industry ties, would be holdings in private companies.

 Sacks, who served as a special government employee in the White House until March, took steps to divest holdings in some companies last year due to his role in the administration.

 People close to Sacks said he likely won't take up the mantle again, although he continues to wield influence through his relationship with Trump. Requirements for divesting holdings could rule out anyone from Anthropic or OpenAI, which are both preparing for blockbuster IPOs. 
 
