 3. Google launches Googlebook to rethink laptop 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Image: Google

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Google is launching a new line of laptops with a starting price of $899, designed for an AI world where people move constantly between phones and computers.

 Why it matters: The tech giant is trying to build a laptop that works as smoothly for premium Android phone users as Apple's MacBook works with the iPhone. The goal is a seamless device ecosystem.

 Googlebook, which will have a low-end price point well below the $1,299 entry-level MacBook Air , isn't meant as a Chromebook replacement or a laptop for every possible buyer.

 "The first version of Googlebook is really about building an amazing laptop for the Android community," top Android executive Sameer Samat said in an interview. Driving the news: The company said today it is opening preorders for five Googlebooks from Acer, Asus, Dell, HP and Lenovo, with starting prices ranging from $899 to $1,299. The first devices hit store shelves Oct. 4 in the U.S.

 The partners are handling the hardware while Google controls the software experience. Samat said the approach gives consumers more choice, with different sizes, designs and form factors, while ensuring that each model works with Android phones in the same way. Catch up quick: Google first announced Googlebook in May as something of an AI sequel to the Chromebook. The company previewed Gemini integration, Magic Pointer, Android app support, phone-file access and the ability to use phone apps from a laptop.

